claude-hud-min was written by cutting claude-hud down to its compact,
single-line layout and dropping everything that layout never used. The code was
largely rewritten — TypeScript to JSDoc-annotated JavaScript, with a different
module split and usage cache — but the behavior it reproduces, and much of the
logic behind that behavior, comes from claude-hud:

  - src/stdin.js   The context percentage model: preferring Claude Code's
                   native `used_percentage`, and the token-based fallback that
                   scales a 16.5% autocompact reserve by raw usage. The
                   constant and the scaling curve are claude-hud's, empirically
                   derived there against `/context`.

  - src/usage.js   The OAuth usage integration: the `/api/oauth/usage`
                   endpoint and its `anthropic-beta: oauth-2025-04-20` header,
                   the credential sources and their precedence, the macOS
                   Keychain service-name scheme (including the hashed suffix
                   for custom config directories), subscription-to-plan-name
                   mapping, and the on-disk caching approach.

  - src/colors.js  The palette and the threshold structure: context green /
                   yellow / red at 70% and 85%, quota blue / magenta / red at
                   75% and 90%, and the filled-and-dimmed block bar.

  - src/render.js  The compact line itself — element order, separators,
                   bracketing of the git segment, reset-time formatting, the
                   80% floor on showing the 7-day quota, and the rule that the
                   plan name moves next to the model when the usage section is
                   absent.

  - src/git.js     Which git facts are shown and what counts as dirty.

